Use case: stylized-concept. Produce ONE square 4 by 4 atlas of sixteen seamless-looking material swatches for a hand-painted CARTOON dungeon game.
Image 1 is the original game illustration and defines the style. Image 2 defines the exact dark cool slate-blue palette; preserve that palette. Do not draw the game scene or its interface.

Crucial style: SIMPLE, SOPHISTICATED CARTOON SURFACE PAINTING like the walls in image 1. Large rounded irregular color patches and broad planes, with clean selective stylized chips. Each swatch has roughly 8 to 15 large quiet painted shapes. Smooth interiors. Almost NO small-scale surface detail. NOT realistic. NO granular texture, NO painterly stipple, NO repeated tiny polygons, NO dense mineral noise, NO mottled lace-like patterns. At game resolution each stone should read as a broad colored plane with just one or two characterful details. Avoid glossy plastic or pure vector gradients.

Layout exactly sixteen equal squares, four columns and four rows, touching edge-to-edge with no frames, labels or outer margins. Every square is a flat full-bleed sample of surface material. No actual stone-block outline, brick edges, grout grid, perspective or cast shadows around the swatches. No text.

Top two rows: eight wall surface variants, all approximately the SAME cool slate-blue average value:
1 calm organic planes with a small shallow nick at lower left;
2 broad smooth worn area with one irregular rim;
3 gentle rounded stone layering, one tiny chip at upper right;
4 a short dark uneven natural crack, integrated into the stone with a subtle edge highlight;
5 two small shallow rounded pits at off-center locations;
6 calm subtly different broad shapes, nearly intact;
7 a compact dark chipped crevice containing a TINY violet luminous sliver (under 5% area);
8 a different small worn crevice containing tiny violet light at a different location.
The corruption is buried inside dark stone fractures, not drawn over the surface. No pale purple branching line symbol. Mostly neutral stone.

Bottom two rows: eight FLOOR surface variants. Same dark-blue family, only a very slight value difference between swatches. Quiet flat worn stone. Each independently composed with different broad soft planes, and only one understated detail: small chip, short hairline crack, smoothed area, faint shallow depression, two tiny pits, worn patch, short weathered nick, nearly intact stone. Do not fill every square with damage. Do not let color differences create a checkerboard when mixed. Floor material is quieter than walls.

Every swatch must differ in the shape and position of its broad color areas. No copied motif, no systematic diagonal stripes. Light consistently from upper left, restrained, with no full-square brightness gradient. Opaque RGB image. No wood, no objects, no torches, no runes, no logos.
